  "evidence": "TEXT_C: Massive NHS and social care expansions (£8bn year one rising to £28bn; free personal care; mental health access in 28 days); extensive redistribution (wealth tax, higher benefits, free school meals, rent controls, 150,000 social homes/year); liberal justice and policing reforms (end routine stop-and-search, decriminalise sex work, presumption against short prison terms); pro-migration measures (end hostile environment, safe asylum routes, voting rights for visa holders) and path to rejoin the EU/cancel Trident. TEXT_D: Strong authority/sovereignty posture (40,000 extra police, zero-tolerance, more stop and search, mandatory life for repeat violent offenders, leave ECHR if needed, patriotic curriculum); loyalty/national preference (freeze non‑essential immigration, detain/deport illegal entrants, prioritise locals in social housing, buy British, higher defence spending); care framed for in-group (eliminate NHS waiting lists while free at point of use, pharmacy‑first, patient vouchers); proportionality/merit cues (raise income tax threshold to make work pay, stronger work requirements with two‑strike rule, five‑year residency for benefits).",
  "reasoning": "Given a profile that prioritises Care but also places high weight on Authority and Loyalty, TEXT_D better matches the blend by coupling an explicit law‑and‑order/sovereignty agenda and national preference with a promise to fix NHS waits. TEXT_C strongly advances Care and Equality but repeatedly conflicts with the electorate’s elevated Authority and Loyalty priorities via liberal justice, expansive migration rights, and EU re‑alignment.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_D"
}
